orig_string Retain original string for user

    def __init__(self, val, unit="", origin=None):
        # ... your existing setup ...
        self._origin = origin if origin else str(val)
        # ...

    @property
    def display(self) -> str:
        '''Formatted representation for human readability.'''
        # This could eventually hold your "prettify" logic
        return f"{self._origin} -> {self}"

Keep it simple; just the information the user type in; do not try to capture all the
later operations, as that can be done by the persistence engine.

Mike's comments:

    Why this is the "Right" Approach:

        Debugging Traceability: When you look at an error log, seeing
        Num('0.0(2)-0.0(2)j') is infinitely more helpful than seeing the evaluated mpc
        object.

        The "Prettify" Property: You mentioned changing dump() to a property. By having
        _origin stored, you can make dump or display intelligently switch contexts.

            Verbose mode: Show the origin + evaluated value + unit.

            Concise mode: Show just the evaluated value.

        User-Defined Logic: You can decide on the fly if the origin is what the user
        typed or a derived expression. If a Num is created via an operation, the
        "origin" could be the expression f"{a} + {b}", effectively giving you a free,
        lightweight expression tree for free.
